+#### Natarajan's Method
+**Definition**: A method addressing interior ambiguities in Marching Cubes by comparing the scalar field value at critical points with vertex values [S2].
+
+**Pitfall**: The table remains incomplete and cannot correctly represent all trilinear interpolant behaviors in the cube interior [S2].
+
+**History**: Proposed by Natarajan in 1994 [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The lookup table approach was introduced by William E. Lorensen and Harvey E. Cline in their 1987 paper "Marching Cubes: A High Resolution 3D Surface Construction Algorithm" [S1, S2, S3]. The method was designed to efficiently extract isosurfaces from volumetric data by precomputing the topological connections for all 256 possible vertex sign configurations. The ambiguity handling problem was identified shortly after, as the standard table produces non-manifold geometries in specific saddle-point cases. Subsequent variants, such as SnapMC, have been developed to handle edge cases where the isosurface intersects grid vertices exactly, using field perturbation to maintain topological consistency [S1].
+The standard lookup table approach was introduced by Williams et al. in 1985 [10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling]. The ambiguity problem in the original 256-case table was identified by Nielson and Hamann, who proposed additional cases to resolve certain ambiguities [S2]. In 1994, Natarajan observed that ambiguity could also occur in the interior of the cube, proposing a method based on critical point comparison, though his table remained incomplete [S2]. In 1995, Chernyaev proposed the Marching Cubes 33 algorithm, presenting a table of 33 cases (32 geometrically distinct) capable of representing all possible behaviors of the trilinear interpolant [S2]. This was later proven correct by Nielson in 2003 [S2].
 
